  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period of \nbreezy to strong NE winds through early next week with \nstrongest winds along the coast, especially south of Cape \nHatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, combined with \nupcoming King Tide cycle lead to additional minor coastal \nimpacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore have increased yet again to\n8-10 ft, and these higher seas will continue today, and only\nvery slowly subside through the coming week, and likely remain\nabove 6 ft through mid week. This will continue to bring the \nthreat of ocean overwash to vulnerable areas where the dune \nstructure has been compromised, especially around the times of \nhigh tide. For ocean-side: high tide is between 6-7 AM this \nmorning, and 630-730 PM this evening. Soundside high tides \nlag from 2 to 3 hours behind the oceanside tides. The National \nParks Service and NCDOT have reported substantial beach erosion \nfrom south Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant overwash \noccurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous road \nconditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from the Marc \nBasnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nThe region is also entering a King Tide cycle this weekend that will \nbuild and peak early to mid next week. With a trend in higher\nseas coinciding with the building tides next week, coastal\nflooding will be likely well into next week. Even with winds\ndiminishing by mid week, \"sunny day\" coastal flooding across \nmuch of the tidal regions of ENC around times of high tide will\ncontinue through mid week. The areas most susceptible to street\nflooding will be low lying areas along the Neuse and Bay rivers\nas well as Downeast Carteret County to North Topsail Beach. The\nmorning high tide will be the highest of the daily tides this \nweekend and next week with the King Tide peaking TUE and WED.\n\nHave extended the coastal flood advisories through noon Tuesday,\nthough with aforementioned King Tides lasting through mid week,\nlater shifts will likely need to extend advisories further out \nin time. In addition, if seas increae any further than \ncurrently forecast, a period of coastal flood warnings may be \nneeded for parts of the OBX, and will need to monitor trends. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler and drier air mass will \nremain with us through much of the extended period with near\naverage highs, to slightly below avg lows, esp mid week when\nhigh pres builds and good radiational cooling yet again sets up\nwith lows well down into the 40s with some 30s again possible. \nMaximum temperatures through the period are expected to remain \nin the mid 60s (OBX) to lower 70s range.",
